// batch w: Austerlitz, Borodino, Leipzig, Waterloo, Nova Orleans, Chacabuco

Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "grande armée": "Napoleon's army. It was born in 1805 from the army that had been waiting at Boulogne to invade England. In 1812 it grew to more than half a million men, of many nationalities.",
  "corpo de exército": "A large unit that groups several divisions, with its own infantry, cavalry and artillery. Napoleon marched with his corps apart, but close enough to join together before the battle.",
  "guarda imperial": "Napoleon's elite troops, made up of the bravest veterans. It was the last reserve: it entered only at the end, to deliver the final blow. That is why it mattered so much when it fell back.",
  "bateria": "A group of cannons that fire together, under the same command.",
  "grande bateria": "Dozens of cannons set side by side, to hammer one point of the enemy line before the infantry attack. It was a trademark of Napoleon, who began his career as an artillery officer.",
  "flechas": "Earthwork fortifications shaped like an arrowhead (flèche, in French), open at the back. The Russians built three of them at Borodino to protect their left wing.",
  "coluna de ataque": "A formation in which soldiers march in narrow, deep blocks, with many ranks. It gives strength for the shock, but exposes the men in front to fire from cannons and from infantry in line.",
  "milícia": "A force made up of citizens who take up arms when needed, without being professional soldiers. It usually has less training than the regular army.",
  "armistício": "An agreement to stop fighting for a time, without yet being peace. It serves to negotiate or to let the army catch its breath.",
  "confederação do reno": "An alliance of German states under Napoleon's protection, created in 1806. It gave him soldiers and a shield against Austria and Prussia. It disappeared after Leipzig.",
  "granadeiros": "Elite infantry. The name comes from the soldiers who once threw grenades and were chosen from among the tallest and strongest. In Napoleon's Guard, the grenadiers were the most prestigious corps.",
  "terra queimada": "A tactic of destroying or carrying off everything the enemy can use (food, shelter, fodder) when retreating. The invader advances into an empty country and goes hungry.",
  "parapeito": "A low wall of earth or stone in front of a position. The soldier behind it fires standing up, but is protected from enemy fire.",
  "baioneta": "A blade fixed to the tip of a rifle, for hand-to-hand fighting. A bayonet charge is an attack at a run, without stopping to fire.",
  "tratado de gante": "The treaty that ended the War of 1812 between the United States and Great Britain, signed on 24 December 1814. It only took effect once ratified by both governments.",
  "realistas": "In Spanish America, those who defended the king of Spain, as opposed to the patriots, who wanted independence.",
  "patriotas": "In Spanish America, those who fought for independence from Spain.",
  "granadeiros a cavalo": "A cavalry regiment created by San Martín in 1812. It was the shock troop of the Army of the Andes.",
  "escaramuça": "A short, small fight, before or during a larger battle."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  austerlitz: {
    war: "War of the Third Coalition (1805)",
    hook: "Napoleon deliberately gave up the strongest ground on the field, waited for the enemy to occupy it and, at the height of the battle, took it back.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "On 2 December 1804, Napoleon had crowned himself Emperor of the French in the cathedral of Notre-Dame. A year later, on the same day, he was in the middle of Moravia, today in the Czech Republic, in a battle that could cost him his throne. Europe had been at war with France, almost without a pause, since 1792. The peace with Great Britain, signed at Amiens in 1802, had been short-lived: in 1803 the war began again.",
        "The British gathered allies in the Third Coalition: Russia, Austria and others. Napoleon had an army of some 150,000 men camped at Boulogne, on the coast of the English Channel, ready to invade England. When Austria moved, he turned everything to the east. This army came to be called the [[Grande Armée|grande armée]], with about 350,000 men divided into seven [[army corps|corpo de exército]].",
        { h: "The road to Austerlitz" },
        { timeline: [
          ["25 Sept. 1805", "About 200,000 Frenchmen begin to cross the Rhine, on a front of some 260 km."],
          ["20 Oct. 1805", "At Ulm, the Austrian general Mack surrenders with about 23,000 men, almost without a battle."],
          ["21 Oct. 1805", "At Trafalgar, the Franco-Spanish fleet is defeated by the British. On land, Napoleon keeps winning."],
          ["Nov. 1805", "Napoleon occupies Vienna and gets the bridges over the Danube intact, along with some 100,000 muskets and 500 cannons."],
          ["2 Dec. 1805", "Battle of Austerlitz."]
        ]},
        "Napoleon's situation, however, was more fragile than it seemed. He was far from home, with his supply lines stretched thin. Prussia, still neutral, threatened to enter the war against him. He needed a quick and complete victory, and he needed the enemy to agree to fight."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Napoleon Bonaparte, 36 years old" },
        "An artillery officer born in Corsica, a general at 24, an emperor at 35. He knew the enemy had more men and more cannons. To make up for it, he made a plan like a piece of theater: he showed weakness so as to be attacked where he wanted. He sent General Savary to the tsar with peace proposals, let hesitation show and abandoned the Pratzen plateau, the best ground on the field. The goal was to make the allies believe he feared the battle.",
        { h: "Alexander I of Russia, 27 years old, and Francis II of Austria" },
        "The young tsar and the Austrian emperor accompanied the army. Alexander and his youngest officers wanted a battle and saw in Napoleon a general who was hesitating. Their haste was the Frenchman's best ally.",
        { h: "Kutuzov, 60 years old, and Weyrother" },
        "The old Russian general Mikhail Kutuzov, who commanded the allied army, wanted to retreat to the Carpathians and wait for reinforcements. He was outvoted. The battle plan was written by the Austrian Weyrother, and the tsar took effective command away from Kutuzov, and it went to Weyrother. Kutuzov remained commander on paper and led one of the columns.",
        { h: "The marshals" },
        "On the French side, Soult (IV Corps) would deliver the decisive blow. Davout (III Corps) had to hold the south, Lannes (V Corps) and Murat (cavalry) the north. The [[Imperial Guard|guarda imperial]] and Bernadotte's I Corps stayed in reserve. On the Russian side, Prince Bagration commanded the allied right flank, to the north."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers vary by source. The French had between 65,000 and 75,000 men, with about 157 cannons. But some 7,000 of them, from Davout's corps, were still marching up from the south. The allies had between 73,000 and 89,000 men, some 70% of them Russian, and about 318 cannons. A common count is about 73,000 French against 85,000 allies.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The field lies some 10 km southeast of Brno. Its center is the Pratzen plateau, a gentle, broad hill. To the north are the hills of Santon and Žuráň, beside the Olmütz road. To the south, the streams and villages of Telnitz and Sokolnitz, and the frozen Satschan ponds. Between the high ground and the French side runs the Goldbach stream.",
        { h: "The French plan: the bait" },
        { formation: true },
        "Napoleon left the plateau empty and hid most of his army behind the Bosenitz stream, on low ground, out of sight. He fortified the Santon hill, in the north, with 18 cannons. And he deliberately weakened the right wing, in the south, to invite the allies to attack it. Davout, who was coming from Vienna, was to hold that wing. He had about 5,000 men at the start, which grew to 12,500, against Buxhoeveden's roughly 43,000. Davout marched some 110 km in 48 hours to arrive in time.",
        "About two thirds of the French forces were concentrated for the blow at the center, on a front of some 3 km. Success depended on one thing beyond his control: the fog. It had to cover the French climb and lift at the right moment.",
        { h: "The allied plan" },
        "Weyrother's plan, adopted on 1 December, called for attacking the French right wing with four columns, while other supporting attacks went in on the north. The Russian Imperial Guard would stay in reserve and Bagration would guard the allied right. It was a plan that assumed Napoleon would stand still and wait.",
        { h: "Morning: the south" },
        "At around 8 in the morning, the allies attacked Telnitz. The village was defended by the 3rd Line Regiment. Davout's first men arrived, drove out the allies and, after hussar counterattacks, the French batteries held the rest. The most contested point was Sokolnitz, where Langeron's bombardment drove out the French and Friant's division ended up retaking the village.",
        "The allies were attacking more slowly than planned. Part of the cavalry had to be diverted from another wing, which delayed one of the columns. And Kutuzov wanted to hold the plateau, but the tsar ordered his column to leave the heights, against the old general's will. The allied center thinned out.",
        { h: "The blow: Pratzen" },
        "At around 8:45, the fog was beginning to lift. Napoleon asked Soult how long his men would need to reach the top. According to the traditional account, Soult answered that it would take less than twenty minutes. Napoleon waited about fifteen minutes more and gave the order.",
        "Saint-Hilaire's division climbed the slope in the fog, and the sun came out just as they reached the top. The allied commanders on the heights were caught by surprise. There was more than an hour of hard fighting across the hills, as Austrian units from the second column came in. To the north, Vandamme's division attacked the vineyard of Staré Vinohrady and broke several allied battalions. Napoleon moved his command post from the Žuráň hill to the chapel of Saint Anthony, on the plateau.",
        "Grand Duke Constantine, the tsar's brother, sent the Russian Imperial Guard against Vandamme. The French lost a flag, from a battalion of the 4th Line Regiment. Napoleon then sent in the heavy cavalry of his Guard. There was a great cavalry clash, in which Drouet's division, of I Corps, served as a shelter for the French horsemen, and the Guard's horse artillery caused heavy losses. The Russians broke and were pursued.",
        { h: "North and south, after the blow" },
        "In the north, Liechtenstein's heavy cavalry attacked Kellermann's, and the cuirassier divisions of d'Hautpoul and Nansouty won after a long melee. Lannes pushed Bagration off the field. Murat did not want to pursue, and Napoleon called off the attack.",
        "In the south, Saint-Hilaire and part of Davout's corps broke through at Sokolnitz. Kienmayer, Langeron and Buxhoeveden fled. Dokhturov's soldiers, who were covering the retreat, were bombarded near Augezd. The ice of the Satschan ponds broke, and men and cannons sank."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The allies lost about 36,000 of their 89,000 men (some 38%), counting dead, wounded and prisoners, as well as 186 cannons and 45 flags. The French lost about 9,000 of 66,000 (some 13%). Historians' counts give the allies between 27,000 and 36,000 casualties, and the French somewhere between 8,500 and 9,300.",
        "Austria asked for a truce, signed on 4 December. On 26 December, the Treaty of Pressburg took the Austrians out of the war: they recognized the loss of lands in Italy and Germany, ceded Venice to the Kingdom of Italy and paid 40 million francs. The Russians were given free passage to go home.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "In 1806, Napoleon created the [[Confederation of the Rhine|confederação do reno]], a cushion of German states between France and the east, and the [[Holy Roman Empire|sacro império]], about a thousand years old, ceased to exist: Francis II remained only as emperor of Austria. Prussia, worried about French influence in Germany, went to war in 1806. Austerlitz also did not settle everything: Great Britain and Russia continued the war.",
        "Historians place it among Napoleon's tactical masterpieces, alongside Cannae and Gaugamela: a plan of bait and blow, carried out with a smaller army and on ground of his choosing. Napoleon gave two million francs in gold to the senior officers and 200 francs to each soldier, and adopted the orphans of the dead.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The name 'Battle of the Three Emperors' comes from Napoleon, Alexander I and Francis II. But some authors point out that Francis was not on the battlefield, and that Napoleon's own letter to Josephine speaks of the 'two emperors' commanding the enemy army." },
        { note: "The legend of the ice: in Napoleon's account, the French cannons broke the ice of the ponds and thousands of Russians drowned. Historians consider this exaggerated. The figures for the dead range from 200 to 2,000 depending on the source, and when the ponds were drained, days later, only two or three bodies and some 150 horses were found. Tsar Alexander himself later acknowledged that the episode happened." },
        { note: "The 'Sun of Austerlitz' is a well-established image, but a legendary one. What the sources actually record is the fog that covered the climb and began to lift at the very hour Napoleon needed it." },
        { note: "Tolstoy made Austerlitz the end of the first volume of War and Peace, and a scene from the battle is carved on the Arc de Triomphe, in Paris. The bicentenary, in 2005, caused controversy because the French government took part in no ceremony." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  borodino: {
    war: "French invasion of Russia (1812)",
    hook: "The bloodiest day of the Napoleonic Wars: about 70,000 dead and wounded in a single day, and neither side truly came out the winner.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "On 24 June 1812, Napoleon crossed the Niemen river and invaded Russia at the head of a [[Grande Armée|grande armée]] of more than 450,000 men in the first wave, which later grew to well over 600,000, from many nations. Tsar Alexander I had broken with Napoleon's system, and Napoleon wanted to force him back into it with a quick, great battle.",
        "But the Russians did not give him that battle. General Barclay de Tolly kept retreating, trading space for time, and the French army wore itself down through marches, disease and hunger. In August, at Smolensk, there was a two-day battle, but the Russian armies escaped encirclement. After Smolensk, the tsar removed Barclay from command and put old Kutuzov in his place. The balance of forces had fallen from some 3 to 1 to some 5 to 4.",
        { h: "The road to Borodino" },
        { timeline: [
          ["24 June 1812", "Napoleon crosses the Niemen."],
          ["16 to 18 Aug.", "Battle of Smolensk: the Russians escape and keep retreating."],
          ["5 to 6 Sept.", "Fight for the Shevardino redoubt, an outpost of the Russian left. The French take the position, with heavy losses on both sides."],
          ["7 Sept. 1812", "Battle of Borodino."]
        ]},
        "The Russians use the [[Julian calendar|calendário juliano]], twelve days behind ours. That is why, in Russian books, the battle is dated 26 August."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Napoleon, 43 years old" },
        "At the height of his power and, that day, with a cold, according to some accounts. He wanted a decisive battle, the kind that ended wars. Instead of maneuvering, he attacked the Russians' fortified positions head on.",
        { h: "Kutuzov, 66 years old" },
        "A veteran of many wars against the Turks and of Austerlitz, where he had been against the battle. He chose to stop and fight in a fortified position, some 120 km from Moscow. After the battle, he declared victory, and Soviet books painted him as a tactical master. Modern historians debate how much of the fighting was actually directed by him.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Russian side, Barclay de Tolly commanded the right and the center, and Prince Bagration the left wing, which took the heaviest blow. Raevsky defended the great redoubt in the center. On the French side, Marshals Davout and Ney attacked the Russian left, the Viceroy of Italy, Eugène de Beauharnais, the center, and the Polish prince Poniatowski, the far left, near Utitsa. The cavalry was Murat's."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Russians had about 155,000 men, with 637 cannons, plus some 43,000 irregulars who did not fight. The French had about 128,000, with 587 cannons. The French Imperial Guard, some 18,500 men, never entered combat.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The Russian position formed an arc of some 8 km, from the Moskva river to the village of Utitsa, passing along the Kolocha stream. There were woods on the left and in the center, which hindered the French advance, and the Kolocha had steep banks. In the center-right stood Raevsky's great redoubt, of earth, open at the back, with about 19 cannons. On the left, Bagration's three [[flèches|flechas]], also open at the back.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        { formation: true },
        "At 6 in the morning, a French [[grand battery|grande bateria]] of about 100 cannons opened fire on the Russian center. At around 7:30, Davout's men took the three flèches. Bagration counterattacked, and Ney retook them. There were seven French assaults in all, and the flèches changed hands several times. During this time, Platov's Cossacks found a ford on the Russian right and asked permission for a raid.",
        "At around 11, Bagration was wounded in the leg by splinters from a cannonball. He stayed on the field for a while, but the news that the commander of the left wing had fallen spread and brought down the morale of the 2nd Army. Dokhturov took command of that sector. (Bagration died of the wound on 24 September.) To the south, Poniatowski's Poles took Utitsa, which the Russians set on fire, and General Tuchkov was mortally wounded in a counterattack. Kutuzov refused to send reinforcements there.",
        "Near noon, Uvarov and Platov, with some 8,000 horsemen and 12 cannons, without infantry, went around and reached the rear of Eugène's IV Corps, which had to fall back. The raid was seen at the time as a failure, but it delayed the French attack by some two hours. That gave the Russians time to reorganize and bring up reinforcements. Some historians think this also influenced Napoleon's decision about the Guard.",
        "At 14:00, Napoleon renewed the assault on Raevsky's redoubt, with the divisions of Broussier, Morand and Gérard. The redoubt fell at around 15:30. The Russian general Likhachov was captured and the French general Caulaincourt died in the cuirassiers' charge. The Russian Guard cavalry held back the French advance beyond the redoubt.",
        { h: "The end of the day" },
        "Several marshals and generals, among them Ney, Berthier and Rapp, asked Napoleon to send in the Guard. He did not. He said he was sure of winning without it, and some accounts speak of illness. The Russian Guard, by contrast, stood still under the fire of the French cannons from 16:00 to 18:00. At night, both armies were exhausted. Kutuzov withdrew in good order on the 8th."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The losses are much debated. The French lost somewhere between 28,000 and 35,000 men, including 49 generals, depending on the count. The Russians, between 44,000 and 52,000, including 22 to 29 generals, depending on the source. Added together, that is some 68,000 to 73,000 in one day, the record of the Napoleonic wars. The second, Waterloo, had about 55,000.",
        "Most historians describe it as a draw or a French Pyrrhic victory: the French kept the field and Napoleon occupied Moscow on 14 September, but Russia did not surrender. Kutuzov, at Fili, on 13 September, decided to give up the city to save the army.",
        "Moscow was deserted and soon caught fire. Who ordered it burned is debated; the governor, Rostopchin, is the traditional suspect. Napoleon waited about five weeks for a peace proposal from the tsar, which never came. He left Moscow on 19 October, lost the battle of Maloyaroslavets and had to go back by the same devastated road, facing the [[scorched earth|terra queimada]] left by the Russians. The snow and cold of November, the hunger and the attacks of Cossacks and peasants did the rest.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Of the more than 600,000 men who entered Russia, only about 110,000 to 120,000 returned, according to estimates. The Russian campaign broke the Grande Armée and, the following year, Europe rose up against Napoleon, as far as Leipzig. Napoleon himself wrote that the French showed themselves worthy of victory and the Russians worthy of being invincible.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Who won? Kutuzov declared himself the winner, partly for political reasons. Soviet books painted him as a tactical master. More recent historians, such as Riehn and Mikaberidze, say that Bagration and Barclay did most of the fighting. Chandler argues that Kutuzov kept control, though he admits the generals made many mistakes. Some also say that Napoleon's decision not to use the Guard spared the Russians a disaster like Austerlitz." },
        { note: "The name: the French call it the Battle of the Moskva (la Moskova), after the river, and Napoleon gave Ney the title Prince of the Moskva because of it. The British officer Robert Wilson, who accompanied the Russian army, called it the Battle of the Generals, because of the number of generals killed and wounded." },
        { note: "In culture: Tolstoy describes it in War and Peace, Tchaikovsky wrote the 1812 Overture and Lermontov a poem. Russia reenacts the battle every year, and the painter Franz Roubaud made a panorama of it in 1912, for the centenary." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  leipzig: {
    war: "War of the Sixth Coalition (1813)",
    hook: "More than half a million soldiers, four days, and a bridge blown up too early: the battle that drove Napoleon out of Germany.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1812, Napoleon invaded Russia with about 600,000 men and lost almost all of them. In Spain, Wellington won at Vitoria, in 1813. Prussia and Russia united against France in the Sixth Coalition, and Germany, which Napoleon dominated, began to stir.",
        "Napoleon raised another army, large but made up of young, inexperienced recruits. He won at Lützen (2 May) and Bautzen (20 and 21 May). Then came an [[armistice|armistício]], during which the allies prepared. The allies agreed, at Trachenberg castle, on a plan: avoid Napoleon himself, fall back when he advanced and attack his marshals. On 12 August, Austria joined the coalition.",
        { h: "The road to Leipzig" },
        { timeline: [
          ["May 1813", "Napoleon wins at Lützen (2 May) and Bautzen (20 and 21 May) with an army of young recruits."],
          ["12 Aug. 1813", "Austria enters the war against France."],
          ["26 and 27 Aug.", "Napoleon wins at Dresden."],
          ["Aug. and Sept.", "The allies beat his marshals at Großbeeren, Kulm, Katzbach and Dennewitz, as the plan required."],
          ["16 to 19 Oct. 1813", "Battle of Leipzig."]
        ]},
        "In the autumn, the allies closed the circle around him. The Saxon and Westphalian soldiers who fought with the French were showing signs of revolt, and some had already switched sides at Großbeeren and Dennewitz."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Napoleon, 44 years old" },
        "He held a central position: he had Leipzig and its bridges, and could send troops from one sector to another faster than the allies. He hoped to beat one army at a time, before the others arrived, as he had so many times before. He did not manage it.",
        { h: "Schwarzenberg, Blücher and Bernadotte" },
        "Prince Karl von Schwarzenberg, an Austrian, commanded the largest army, that of Bohemia, and was the overall commander of the coalition. The Prussian Blücher, aged 70, commanded the Army of Silesia, and was the most aggressive. The Army of the North belonged to the Swede Charles John, who had been one of Napoleon's marshals under the name Bernadotte. General Bennigsen brought the Army of Poland, a Russian reserve. The three sovereigns, Tsar Alexander I, Emperor Francis I of Austria and King Frederick William III of Prussia, accompanied the army.",
        { h: "Poniatowski" },
        "Prince Józef Poniatowski, a Pole, commanded the Poles on Napoleon's side, and Napoleon promoted him to marshal of France during the battle. His end, on the last day, is one of the symbols of Leipzig."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "On the 16th and 17th, the allies had about 257,000 men and 1,145 cannons, against about 177,000 French and 700 cannons. On the 18th and 19th, with reinforcements, they rose to about 359,000 men and 1,500 cannons, against about 195,000 and 900. In all, there were some 560,000 soldiers, and Russia had 150,000, Austria 115,000, Prussia 90,000 and Sweden 30,000. On the French side, only some 160,000 were French: the rest were Poles, Italians, Neapolitans, Saxons and men from Baden, Württemberg, Hesse and Westphalia.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The Pleiße and Parthe rivers join at Leipzig and divide the ground into four sectors. Whoever held the city and its bridges, in this case Napoleon, could shift troops between the sectors faster. To the west lay Lindenau, to the south Liebertwolkwitz and Wachau, to the southeast Probstheida and to the east Paunsdorf and Schönefeld. At Möckern, in the north, there was a dike some 4 meters high along the Elster river.",
        { h: "16 October: the day of the draw" },
        "In the south, Napoleon wanted to attack between the Pleiße and the Parthe. The Austrian Merveldt attacked toward Connewitz and Dölitz, and ended up wounded and captured. At Liebertwolkwitz, Klenau's Austrians drove out the French and were counterattacked. Napoleon ordered the formation of a [[grand battery|grande bateria]] under Drouot, of about 150 cannons, and Murat launched about 10,000 French, Italian and Saxon horsemen in columns. The smaller allied cavalry pushed them back, and they were saved by the dragoons of the Guard.",
        "In the north, at Möckern, the Russians of Langeron and the Prussians of Yorck, of the Army of Silesia, fought Marmont's VI Corps. The losses were about 9,000 allies and 7,000 French, plus 2,000 French prisoners. The day ended in a draw: the allies lost about 30,000 and the French some 25,000.",
        { h: "17 October: the pause" },
        "It was a calm day, with only two small fights. In these days, Napoleon tried to negotiate: he sent back, on parole, General Merveldt, whom he had captured, with a proposal for an armistice (handing over the fortresses of the Oder and the Vistula in exchange for a withdrawal behind the Saale river). The three sovereigns refused. Sources differ on the exact day. The reinforcements were very unequal: the French received some 14,000 men, from General Reynier, and the allies about 145,000, from the Army of the North and from Bennigsen.",
        { h: "18 October: the encirclement" },
        "The allies closed the circle. The bloodiest point was Probstheida, where Prussian and Russian attacks and Raevsky's charge failed against the artillery of the French Guard. At Breitenfeld, Charles John and Blücher met. At Paunsdorf, the Army of the North used the Congreve rockets of a British brigade, and about 5,000 to 6,000 Saxons of Reynier's VII Corps changed sides in the middle of the fight, along with Württemberg cavalry. The French line fell back. At Lindenau, in the west, Bertrand's IV Corps drove out Gyulay's Austrians and kept the road for the retreat open.",
        { h: "19 October: the bridge" },
        "The allies discovered the evacuation at 7 in the morning and attacked between 8 and 9. The rear guard, with about 30,000 men under Oudinot and other commanders, fought street by street in Leipzig. Even the Swedish riflemen joined the final assault.",
        "The only bridge over the White Elster, which the French needed to cross, had been prepared to be blown up after everyone had passed. The order was passed from General Dulauloy to a colonel, and from him to a corporal. The corporal lit the fuse at around 1 in the afternoon, when the bridge was still full of Frenchmen and the rear guard was still in the city. The explosion caused panic: thousands died and some 30,000 were captured. Marshal Macdonald swam across the river. Poniatowski, wounded, drowned."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The allies lost about 54,000 men of 360,000 (Schwarzenberg some 34,000, Blücher some 12,000, and Charles John and Bennigsen some 4,000 each). Napoleon lost about 38,000 dead and wounded. The allies captured some 15,000 able-bodied men, 21,000 wounded or sick, 325 cannons and 28 eagles or flags. Six French generals died, 12 were wounded and 36 captured, including Lauriston and Reynier.",
        "French power east of the Rhine was over. Baden, Saxony and Württemberg went over to the coalition's side, and the [[Confederation of the Rhine|confederação do reno]] fell apart. At Hanau, on 30 and 31 October, he still forced his way through against Austrians and Bavarians. On his return to Paris, he is said to have remarked that, a year earlier, all of Europe had marched with him, and now it marched against him.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The coalition invaded France at the beginning of 1814, and Paris fell on 31 March. Napoleon abdicated on 6 April and went to the island of Elba. It was the largest battle in Europe until the First World War, according to many historians, and it is the one Germany remembers as the Völkerschlacht, the beginning of liberation from French tutelage. In 1913, on the centenary, the Monument to the Battle of the Nations was inaugurated in Leipzig.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The name: Battle of the Nations, in French Bataille des Nations and in German Völkerschlacht, because soldiers of many countries fought together, from Russians and Swedes to Poles, Italians and Germans of various states, on both sides." },
        { note: "The 30,000 at the bridge: the number that appears in many books is of prisoners, taken in the city and at the bridge, and it includes many wounded and sick. The allies counted about 15,000 able-bodied men and 21,000 wounded or sick captured in all of Leipzig. How many died in the explosion, the sources do not say with certainty." },
        { note: "The Congreve rockets: the British rocket brigade of the Army of the North helped drive the French out of Paunsdorf. Captain Bogue was killed by a sharpshooter." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  waterloo: {
    war: "The Hundred Days (1815)",
    hook: "Napoleon escaped from Elba, retook France almost without firing a shot and lost everything on a Sunday, at Waterloo.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In April 1814, after Leipzig and the invasion of France, Napoleon abdicated and went to the island of Elba. Europe went back to being ruled by kings, and France again had a Bourbon, Louis XVIII, on the throne. But the new king had little support among Napoleon's old soldiers, and Napoleon found out.",
        "On 26 February 1815, he left Elba with about a thousand men and landed in France on 1 March. The regiments sent to arrest him changed sides, one by one. He entered Paris on 20 March, without a battle, and the king fled. The Hundred Days began. On 13 March, the Congress of Vienna had already declared him an outlaw, and on 17 March the United Kingdom, Russia, Austria and Prussia mobilized against him, in the Seventh Coalition.",
        { h: "The road to Waterloo" },
        { timeline: [
          ["15 June 1815", "Napoleon crosses the Belgian border, near Charleroi, to attack the allied armies before they join up."],
          ["16 June", "At Ligny, he defeats Blücher's Prussians. At the same time, Ney fights Wellington at Quatre Bras, which Wellington holds."],
          ["17 June", "The Prussians retreat north, toward Wavre. Wellington falls back to the ridge of Mont-Saint-Jean, near Waterloo. Napoleon sends Grouchy, with 33,000 men, after the Prussians. It rains all night."],
          ["18 June 1815", "Battle of Waterloo."]
        ]},
        "Napoleon's idea was simple: beat the two allied armies separately, before they met. He did well at Ligny. But he let the Prussians escape, and Grouchy lost sight of them."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Napoleon, 45 years old" },
        "He was back in power, with an army of veterans and no forced conscription in this campaign. He gave Ney tactical command of a good part of the army. One decision that weighed heavily was to wait for the ground to dry on the morning of 18 June, which delayed the attack and gave the Prussians time.",
        { h: "Wellington, 46 years old" },
        "The Duke of Wellington, who had fought on the Iberian Peninsula and had never faced Napoleon in person, was a master of defense: he hid his troops behind a ridge so that the enemy could neither see them nor hit them with artillery. His army was mixed, with British, Hanoverians, Dutch, Belgians, Brunswickers and Nassauers.",
        { h: "Blücher, 72 years old" },
        "The old Prussian marshal had just lost at Ligny and had been thrown from his horse. Even so, he promised Wellington he would come with three corps, and he came.",
        { h: "Ney and Grouchy" },
        "Marshal Ney, 'the bravest of the brave', was the chief of a large part of the French front line. Grouchy, with about a third of the army, was supposed to keep the Prussians away and failed."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The French had from 72,000 to 74,500 men and some 250 cannons. Wellington had about 68,000 (the most used count), or even 74,000, and 156 cannons, plus some 17,000 left at Halle to protect a flank. The Prussians were about 50,000, with 126 cannons, and arrived gradually during the afternoon. Wellington still had little heavy cavalry, but he gave Uxbridge freedom to use the cavalry as he saw fit.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The ridge of Mont-Saint-Jean crosses the Brussels road. In front of it, Wellington had two fortified farms: Hougoumont, on the right, and La Haye Sainte, in the center. Behind the ridge, the infantry stayed out of sight. The French stood on the other side of a valley, with the Brussels road in the middle.",
        { h: "Morning: the wait" },
        "The night's rain left the ground soaked, and Napoleon waited for it to dry, because of the artillery and the cavalry. Close to 11, he gave the general order: Jérôme would attack Hougoumont, the [[grand battery|grande bateria]] would bombard from 13:00, and d'Erlon and Reille would attack Wellington's left. Meanwhile, Bülow's Prussian IV Corps was slowly leaving Wavre, delayed by a fire.",
        { h: "The day" },
        { formation: true },
        "Hougoumont. The attack began at around 11:30. General Bauduin, who led the first attack, died. Lieutenant Legros broke open the north gate with an axe, and soldiers of the Coldstream Guards and the Scots Guards closed the gate behind the Frenchmen who had entered, who were killed. Napoleon had the house bombarded, and it caught fire, except for the chapel. He threw 33 battalions into it, about 14,000 men. Wellington spent 21 battalions, about 12,000, to hold it. Hougoumont held out to the end.",
        "The grand battery. About 80 cannons opened fire at around noon (the times vary in the sources, from 11:50 to 13:30). At around 13:15, Napoleon saw Prussian columns approaching Lasne, some 6 to 8 km from his right.",
        "D'Erlon's attack. At around 13:30, about 14,000 men of I Corps advanced in [[attack columns|coluna de ataque]], against some 6,000 defenders on Wellington's left. General Picton died soon after ordering a counterattack. The French almost broke through, and at around 14:00 Napoleon seemed to be winning. But the British heavy cavalry brigades, the Household and the Union, with the Scots Greys, under Uxbridge, attacked and drove back the columns. The Greys took the eagle of the 45th of the Line. But, out of control, they lost themselves in the charge: the two heavy brigades lost 1,205 men and 1,303 horses.",
        "Ney's charges. A little before 16:00, Ney saw movement among the wounded at Wellington's center and took it for a retreat. He attacked with cavalry, without infantry alongside. The first wave had some 4,800 sabers. With more cavalry, they reached about 9,000 horsemen in 67 squadrons. Wellington's infantry formed [[squares|quadrado]], and witnesses count 12 assaults, without breaking a single one. Napoleon is said to have remarked that the charge was an hour too early, but he did not stop it. The heavy cavalry of the Guard lost 47% of its men.",
        "The Prussians. Bülow's IV Corps arrived in force at around 16:30. It attacked Plancenoit, on the French right. Napoleon sent the eight battalions of the Young Guard and then two of the Old Guard. Plancenoit changed hands five times. The Young Guard had losses of some 96%, and two thirds of Lobau's corps ceased to exist.",
        "La Haye Sainte. The farm held out for almost the whole afternoon. When the ammunition of the King's German Legion ran out, it fell. The French artillery began to fire canister at point-blank range against the squares. Ney still sent infantry, about 6,500 men, with cavalry, but Uxbridge's cavalry stopped them.",
        { h: "The end: the Guard" },
        "Zieten's Prussian I Corps arrived late in the afternoon. Müffling persuaded him to turn to Wellington's left, and not to go and help Bülow. At around 19:30, the French line was already a horseshoe. Napoleon then played his last card: five battalions of the Middle Guard, with three of the Old Guard in reserve, with Ney in front.",
        "It was a disaster. Chassé's Dutch division attacked the flank of the Guard. A horse artillery battery fired into the flank of the grenadiers, who broke. Maitland's Foot Guards stood up and fired volleys at the chasseurs. Colborne's 52nd counterattacked. The Guard fell back, which, according to tradition, had never happened. The cry 'La Garde recule!' ran along the line, and Wellington ordered the whole army forward. The French army fled. Napoleon was taken from the field by his aides.",
        "According to tradition, Wellington and Blücher met at around 21:00 near the La Belle Alliance farm. Blücher wanted to give the battle its name. Wellington preferred the name of the village of Waterloo, where he had slept and written his report. The French call it Mont-Saint-Jean."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Wellington's army lost about 17,000 men (3,500 dead, 10,200 wounded and 3,300 missing), and Blücher's about 7,000. The French lost from 24,000 to 26,000 dead and wounded, plus 6,000 to 8,000 prisoners, and some 15,000 deserted afterward. About 220 cannons were captured, according to the most common counts. At least 7,000 horses were killed or wounded.",
        "Napoleon reached Paris on 21 June and abdicated between the 22nd and the 24th. The allies entered the capital on 7 July. On 15 July, he surrendered to Captain Maitland, of the British ship HMS Bellerophon, and was sent to Saint Helena, an island in the South Atlantic, where he died in 1821.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the end of the First French Empire and of the wars that began in 1792. Europe followed the design of the Congress of Vienna, and the second Treaty of Paris, of 20 November 1815, imposed harsher terms on France. It was the second bloodiest single day of the Napoleonic wars, after Borodino. And 'to meet your Waterloo' became a byword for a defeat with no way back.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Who won? Wellington, in his report, praised the 'cordial and timely' help of the Prussians. Jomini considered Blücher's arrival the most important cause of the defeat. A modern historian writes that Blücher 'deserves all the credit for preventing a British defeat'. Another argues that the arrival of the Prussians forced Napoleon to hurry and let Wellington go on the offensive. Clausewitz thought that an attack in the morning would have decided the battle before the Prussians arrived." },
        { note: "The causes of the defeat: Jomini pointed to four (Blücher and Grouchy's mistake, the steadiness of the English infantry, the rain that delayed the attack and the deep columns). Clausewitz rejected the idea of chance or treason and spoke of 'enormous errors' and of too much ambition." },
        { note: "'Cambronne's word': the reply attributed to General Cambronne, 'Merde!', when he was asked to surrender, is disputed. Letters to the Times, in 1932, suggest that he was already a prisoner and that whoever said it may have been General Michel. Another phrase attributed to him, 'The Guard dies, but does not surrender', is also disputed." },
        { note: "Wellington said it was 'a near thing' (in English, the nearest run thing he had ever seen). The Lion's Mound, an artificial hill, was made with about 300,000 m³ of earth taken from the ridge at the center of the field, which lowered the road. Victor Hugo lamented that the field was 'disfigured in order to be glorified'. The sentence attributed to Wellington, 'They have changed my battlefield!', has no record." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  novaorleans: {
    war: "War of 1812 (United States against Great Britain)",
    hook: "About 2,000 British casualties against 71 American ones, in a battle fought 15 days after the peace treaty had been signed.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1812, the United States declared war on Great Britain, because of the blockade of American trade, the forced recruitment of sailors and British support for the Native peoples who resisted American expansion. In 1814, after Napoleon's fall, Great Britain could send more troops and burned Washington in August. For the end of the year, it planned an expedition against the coast of the Gulf of Mexico.",
        "The Gulf campaign lasted from September 1814 to February 1815. The objective was to take New Orleans, which controlled the mouth of the Mississippi river and, with it, the trade of the whole American interior. Some historians think the British wanted to keep Louisiana. Others, like Jon Latimer, disagree, and Carr concludes that Great Britain was in a hurry to end the war.",
        { h: "The road to New Orleans" },
        { timeline: [
          ["14 Dec. 1814", "About 980 British sailors and marines, under Captain Lockyer, capture five American gunboats on Lake Borgne."],
          ["23 Dec.", "About 1,800 British soldiers reach the bank of the Mississippi, some 14 km (9 miles) from New Orleans. Jackson attacks them at night, with about 2,100 men, and gains time."],
          ["24 Dec.", "In Europe, the [[Treaty of Ghent|tratado de gante]] is signed. The news takes weeks to arrive."],
          ["25 Dec.", "Pakenham arrives and takes over the British command."],
          ["1 Jan. 1815", "An artillery duel of some three hours. Pakenham calls off the attack."],
          ["8 Jan. 1815", "Battle of New Orleans."]
        ]},
        "The Treaty of Ghent only took effect once ratified by both governments. The United States ratified it on 16 February 1815. Besides, the British minister Bathurst had told Pakenham to continue the war even if he heard rumors of peace."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Andrew Jackson, 47 years old" },
        "A Tennessee militia general, tough and stubborn, nicknamed 'Old Hickory'. He was warned of the British landing by Major Gabriel Villeré, who escaped from an attack on his house. He attacked the British at night, right away, to gain time, and then fell back some 3 km to a good position to defend: the Rodriguez Canal.",
        { h: "Edward Pakenham, 36 years old" },
        "A veteran of Wellington's wars in Spain and his brother-in-law. He arrived two days after the first fight, and found the enemy already entrenched. Instead of looking for another route, he decided to attack head on.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "The British generals Samuel Gibbs and John Keane commanded the two attack columns, and John Lambert was with the reserve. Colonel William Thornton was to cross the river on the west bank. On the American side, there was General John Coffee, of Tennessee, the commanders of the battalions of free men of color, Savary, Lacoste and Daquin, and the privateer Jean Lafitte, with the Baratarians."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers vary. The most used counts give about 5,700 Americans and 8,000 British in the whole campaign. On 8 January, Jackson had 4,698 men on the east bank and 546 on the west. The British attacked with about 5,400 men.",
        "The American force was a mixture: regular soldiers, [[militia|milícia]] from Tennessee, Kentucky (about 2,250 arrived, but only some 550 had weapons) and Louisiana, Mississippi cavalry, two battalions of free men of color (some 500, according to one source), about 60 Choctaw Natives and some 50 Baratarians, who operated the cannons. Tradition says that what decided it were the Kentucky riflemen. Since the 1960s, historians have shown that the role of the militias was smaller, and that the artillery caused most of the British losses.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Jackson's line ran from the Mississippi river, for three quarters of a mile (some 1.2 km), to a cypress swamp. It was an earthen [[parapet|parapeito]], some 2 meters high, behind the Rodriguez Canal, which was 3 to 6 meters wide and 1.2 to 2.4 deep. Enslaved people helped to build it. On the other bank of the river stood a navy battery.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        { formation: true },
        "On 27 December, the American ship Carolina was sunk by a hot shot. On 28 December, a British reconnaissance attempt failed. On 1 January, there was a three-hour artillery duel, and the house that served as Jackson's headquarters was destroyed, but he came out unharmed. Pakenham gave up the attack, at 15:00.",
        "The plan of 8 January had two parts. The main one, the frontal attack on the line, by two columns, Gibbs's and Keane's. The other, on the west bank: Colonel Thornton would cross the river and take the American battery there. The attack began at dawn, in the middle of the fog.",
        "Two errors weighed heavily. Colonel Thomas Mullins, of the 44th, was supposed to bring the ladders and the [[fascines|fascinas]] to cross the canal and climb the parapet, but he halted his regiment at the wrong redoubt. The ladders arrived too late. And the canal that was meant to carry Thornton's boats to the other side of the river collapsed, and the current carried them some 900 meters downstream. Only 560 men arrived, a third of the plan.",
        "On the east bank, the American artillery and rifles tore the columns apart. Pakenham was killed, Gibbs was mortally wounded, and Rennie also died. Keane was wounded. Of about 3,000 men under Gibbs and Keane, some 2,000 fell in less than 30 minutes. The main attack lasted some 30 minutes, and the whole fight some two hours.",
        "On the west bank, Thornton got the better of it: the 560 men drove off the Kentucky and Louisiana militia, who fled, and took the battery. But Lambert, who had taken command, ordered a retreat, and the artillery officer Dickson had estimated that 2,000 men would be needed to hold it."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Americans had 13 dead, 39 wounded and 19 missing or captured: 71 casualties. The British had about 290 dead, 1,260 wounded and 484 missing or captured, around 2,040 (the counts differ a little between sources). The British stayed on the west bank until 9 January. They left the field between 18 and 19 January and embarked again by the 27th. The fleet still took Fort Bowyer, at Mobile, on 12 February, and then the news of the treaty arrived.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The battle did not change the result of the war, which was already settled on paper. But it made Jackson a national hero and gave Americans the feeling of having defeated the British Empire in a 'second war of independence'. 8 January was a federal holiday from 1828 to 1861. Jackson was elected president and took office in 1829. The victory destroyed the Federalist Party, whose delegation from the Hartford Convention reached Washington after the news, and led to the one-party period of the 'Era of Good Feelings'.",
        "Alan Taylor argues that the victory had enormous consequences: it gave Americans primacy on the continent and left the Native nations unprotected.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The myth of the sharpshooters: the song 'The Hunters of Kentucky' was used in Jackson's campaigns in 1824 and 1828 and made the Kentucky rifles famous. But only 800 to 1,200 private rifles were used, and most of the men had muskets. The engineers, the enslaved people who raised the parapets and the trained gunners had their role diminished." },
        { note: "'Beauty and Booty': in 1815, a letter from the politician George Poindexter said the British used 'Booty and Beauty' as a watchword, that is, permission for plunder and abuse. In 1833, five British officers signed a denial. Many consider the matter closed." },
        { note: "The battle also entered popular culture: in 1959, the song by Jimmy Driftwood, recorded by Johnny Horton, was number 1 on the Billboard Hot 100. Statues of Pakenham and Gibbs are in St Paul's Cathedral, in London. Lambert, who took command at New Orleans, would later fight at Waterloo." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  chacabuco: {
    war: "South American wars of independence: the liberation of Chile (1817)",
    hook: "San Martín crossed the Andes with an army and fell on the Spaniards on the other side, in two columns that did not arrive together.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In October 1814, at Rancagua, the Spaniards defeated the Chilean patriots and ended the period of the 'Old Fatherland'. Those who escaped fled to the other side of the Andes, to the province of Cuyo, in present-day Argentina, governed by General José de San Martín.",
        "San Martín had a plan bigger than Chile: to liberate Chile and, from there, attack Peru by sea. To do that, he first had to cross the Andes.",
        { h: "The crossing" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1814 to 1816", "San Martín builds the Army of the Andes at Mendoza. Bernardo O'Higgins and Ramón Freire help organize it. He frees enslaved people, who enlist."],
          ["14 to 23 Jan. 1817", "The army sets out in columns, over several passes, on a front of some 800 km."],
          ["9 Feb. 1817", "The main columns join up at Curimón, on the Chilean side."],
          ["12 Feb. 1817", "Battle of Chacabuco."]
        ]},
        "There were six columns. The main one, with about 2,650 combatants and 7 cannons, crossed by the Los Patos pass with San Martín, O'Higgins and Soler. A second, of some 680 men, went through Uspallata, under Juan Gregorio de Las Heras. Smaller columns, farther north and south, occupied towns such as Copiapó and Coquimbo. The main route was more than 500 km long, with an average height of some 3,000 meters, and some passes went above 4,500 meters.",
        "The army was estimated at about 4,000 soldiers, plus auxiliaries: about 5,400 people in all, according to one reconstruction. It carried about 9,300 mules and some 1,200 to 1,600 horses, and between 16 and 21 pieces of artillery (the sources differ), which the friar Luis Beltrán helped to make and to transport. Only about 4,300 mules and 500 horses reached Chile. Some English-language sources speak of up to a third of the men lost, a figure that other sources do not confirm."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "José de San Martín, 38 years old" },
        "Born in what is today Argentina, he fought in the Spanish army for more than 20 years before returning to America in 1812. He was the brains of the operation: he planned the crossing, the supply and the deception. Through spies and talks with the Pehuenche Natives, he spread the rumor that he would pass farther south. The Pehuenches told this to the Spaniards, who scattered their forces.",
        { h: "Bernardo O'Higgins, 38 years old" },
        "A Chilean, the son of an Irishman who rose to viceroy of Peru, he had been one of the leaders of the Old Fatherland and fought at Rancagua. He was brave, and it was his attack, ahead of time, that became the most debated point of the day.",
        { h: "Soler and the others" },
        "General Miguel Estanislao Soler commanded the right wing. José Matías Zapiola led the squadrons of [[mounted grenadiers|granadeiros a cavalo]] of O'Higgins's column, San Martín's shock cavalry. On the Spanish side, Governor Casimiro Marcó del Pont had troops scattered across the country. Colonel Rafael Maroto, who commanded at Chacabuco, wanted to retreat south, but Marcó del Pont ordered him to block the road to Santiago. Maroto took a defensive position near the Chacabuco farm."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers vary. The Army of the Andes had between 3,500 and 4,000 men in the fight, and the columns of Soler and O'Higgins totaled some 3,500: about 2,000 to 2,100 under Soler and 1,500 under O'Higgins. The [[royalists|realistas]] had between 1,400 and 2,450 men: the Talavera regiment, the Chiloé and Valdivia battalions, and cavalry of the Concordia Hussars and Abascal's Carabineers.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Chacabuco lies north of Santiago, in a valley ringed by hills. The patriots came from the north, down the slopes. The royalists waited on the heights, near the farm, with the city of Santiago behind them.",
        { h: "The plan" },
        { formation: true },
        "On 11 February, San Martín divided about 3,500 men into two columns, one on each side of the hill. Soler would go on the right, by the Montenegro road, to the west. O'Higgins, on the left, by the Cuesta Vieja, to the east. San Martín would attack from the front. The idea was a pincer attack at dawn: Soler would cut off the Spaniards' flank and retreat.",
        "The patriots began to descend at midnight, to attack at dawn. Soler's path was narrower and harder, and he was late. O'Higgins arrived first and attacked, before the orders reached Soler. San Martín sent a messenger to tell Soler to attack at once.",
        "According to the accounts, at around 11:45, O'Higgins ordered the infantry to attack with the bayonet, against San Martín's orders. The cavalry fell into a ravine it had not seen and fell back. O'Higgins and Crámer attacked again, with the cavalry on the royalist right flank and the infantry in the center. Zapiola broke a square of the Talavera and went around the Spaniards' right. A second attack scattered the enemy infantry and cavalry. Soler's troops arrived, enveloped the royalist left and rear and took an artillery position. The royalists formed a square around the farm, and the fight ended in hand-to-hand combat. The battle ended at around 14:00. Maroto escaped on horseback, slightly wounded."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The royalists lost about 500 dead and 600 prisoners. San Martín's official report spoke of 450 dead and 600 prisoners. The patriots had about 12 dead and 120 wounded, according to the most cited counts, and San Martín's report speaks of fewer than 100 casualties. San Martín did not pursue those who fled: about 1,600 soldiers embarked for Peru and were the basis of Osorio's expedition. Some historians think a pursuit would have shortened the war.",
        "The patriots entered Santiago on 14 February. Marcó del Pont fled. An assembly offered the government to San Martín, who refused, and then named O'Higgins Supreme Director of Chile, a post he held until 1823. Chile's independence was proclaimed on 12 February 1818, a year after the battle. What was left was decided at Maipú, in April 1818.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Chacabuco opened the door to Chile and, with it, the sea route to Peru. The crossing of the Andes is often compared to Hannibal's in the Alps. A good part of the success came before the battle, from logistics and deception: the Spaniards, warned by San Martín's rumors, had scattered their forces.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "O'Higgins's early attack is the most debated point. In some accounts, the charge was repulsed with many casualties and only Soler's arrival saved the day. O'Higgins said the royalists had begun to advance against his men." },
        { note: "Who fought? The composition of the Army of the Andes, Argentine or Chilean, is disputed. Estimates of the Chilean share range from about 30% of the troops to about 1,200 soldiers, and no official record survives." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

